+## Relay device packets (`0x4X`)
+
+Packet tags `0x40`–`0x4F` are reserved for relay devices. Bulbs reject these
+tags as unknown, and relays treat the bulb color tags (`0x01`–`0x03`, `0x06`)
+as no-ops, so the two families never misread each other's frames. New
+device-specific packets must take an unassigned tag; a tag never changes
+meaning once it's assigned.
+
+## `0x40` - RelaysUpdate
+
+Continuously-broadcast desired state for every relay device. Relay devices
+share the device id space with bulbs (ids are unique across device types); the
+bitfield covers the entire space, so bits at ids occupied by bulbs (or by
+nothing) are meaningless and ignored by everyone.
+
+A relay with id N reads bit N and sets its relay to that value. Bit N lives at
+`relay_states[N / 8]`, bit position `N % 8` (LSB-first). The packet is pure
+state — no seq, no epoch. It is idempotent: a relay may apply the same packet
+any number of times, so the base station rebroadcasts it continuously and
+packet loss heals on the next frame.
+
+```
+struct RelaysUpdatePacket {
+    relay_states: [u8; 32],   // bit N = desired relay state of device id N
+}
+```
+
+## `0x41` - RelaysLedUpdate
+
+Identical to RelaysUpdate in every way, but the bits drive the relay device's
+status LED instead of the relay.
+
+```
+struct RelaysLedUpdatePacket {
+    led_states: [u8; 32],     // bit N = desired LED state of device id N
+}
+```
+
+### Relay device behavior
+
+Each relay device has two provisioned DEFAULT values: `default_relay` and
+`default_led` (each on/off). At power-on the device applies both defaults
+before the radio comes up. Thereafter each packet type feeds its own fallback
+timer: if no RelaysUpdate is heard for the fallback window (15 s default) the
+relay reverts to `default_relay`; if no RelaysLedUpdate is heard for the same
+window the LED reverts to `default_led`. The timers are independent — a base
+station that only broadcasts RelaysUpdate gets default LED behavior.
+
+The physical button has no function.
+
+Body size: tag + 32 = 33 bytes, within the 70-byte vendor-IE body limit.
+
+Relay devices honor `0x04` BulbCommand (SetConfig / Reboot) addressed by their
+shared-space id, and `0x05` Dfu2Request with `device_type` 0x02.
+
+SetConfig keys are ONE FLAT SPACE across all device types: a key number has
+one meaning everywhere, and devices ignore keys they don't implement. Shared
+concepts reuse the existing keys — `0x0001` device id, `0x0020` fallback_ms,
+`0x0021` wifi_channel apply to bulbs and relays alike (same types). Relay-
+specific keys live at `0x005X`: `0x0050` default_relay (u8 0/1), `0x0051`
+default_led (u8 0/1). New keys must not repurpose assigned numbers.
